{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And all the good of his master in his hand.</b> Since Scripture did not specify what was in his hand, it is reasonable to understand it as referring to what we find that he took in his hand, namely: ״נֶזֶם זָהָב בֶּקַע מִשְׁקָלוֹ וּשְׁנֵי צְמִידִים עֲשָׂרָה זָהָב מִשְׁקָלָם״ (Genesis 24:22). And he called them 'all the good of his master' because Abraham made through them a sign alluding to the shekels and to the Ten Commandments that are on the two tablets, and 'good' means nothing other than Torah. Therefore he called them 'all the good of his master,' for this is the true good. That is why it does not say 'all the property of his master.' And all the allusions contained in them will be explained, with the help of HaShem, in the vicinity [of that verse]."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:32.956421954Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}